 "endLine": 0}}}], "properties": {"source": "INFRA"}}, {"ruleId": "7e82a2263a013e6c", "level": "error", "message": {"text": "[expert] The new _run_review only reads the tail of stderr (last 64KB). Infra-failure detection (_is_infra_failure) relies on this stderr text. If a backend error message appears earlier in stderr (e.g., a long traceback prefix before the actual 'Connection refused' line), the tail may not contain it, causing a non-zero exit to be misclassified as a finding rather than an infra skip. This is a regression from the previous capture_output behavior which captured all stderr. Consider scanning a larger window or the full stderr when the exit code is non-zero."}, "locations": [{"physicalLocation": {"artifactLocation": {"uri": "src/code_forge/eval/runner.py"}, "region": {"startLine": 164, "endLine": 164}}}], "properties": {"source": "L1"}}, {"ruleId": "9f23d4320bd3fe55", "level": "error", "message": {"text": "[adversarial] Test `test_cleanup_on_windows_does_not_reach_for_process_groups` patches `code_forge.eval.runner.os.getpgid` and `code_forge.eval.runner.os.killpg`, but the actual calls to `os.getpgid` and `os.killpg` are inside `code_forge.proc.group_of` and `code_forge.proc.terminate_group_or_child`/`kill_group_or_child` which are imported into the runner module. The patches are ineffective, so the test does not actually simulate Windows behavior. This could lead to undetected cross-platform regressions."}, "locations": [{"physicalLocation": {"artifactLocation": {"uri": "tests/test_cli_eval.py"}, "region": {"startLine": 717, "endLine": 717}}}], "properties": {"source": "L1"}}, {"ruleId": "8b8b1890216d2bdb", "level": "error", "message": {"text": "[adversarial] Test `test_the_windows_teardown_never_names_the_signal_it_lacks` patches `code_forge.eval.runner.os.name` to `'nt'` but the `os.name` check inside `group_of` is in the `code_forge.proc` module, which is not patched. The test also deletes `signal.SIGKILL` globally, but the code path that uses `signal.SIGKILL` is not reached because `pgid` is `None` (the mock proc has no real PID). The test does not actually verify that the SIGKILL reference is avoided on Windows."}, "locations": [{"physicalLocation": {"artifactLocation": {"uri": "tests/test_cli_eval.py"}, "region": {"startLine": 761, "endLine": 761}}}], "properties": {"source": "L1"}}, {"ruleId": "15c1f15a5ebf537c", "level": "error", "message": {"text": "[adversarial] Test `test_terminate_and_reap_on_windows_names_no_absent_signal` patches `code_forge.mcp_jobs.os.name` to `'nt'` but the `os.name` check inside `group_of` is in the `code_forge.proc` module, which is not patched. The test also deletes `signal.SIGKILL` globally, but the code path that uses `signal.SIGKILL` is not reached because `pgid` is `None`. The test does not actually verify that the async teardown avoids the missing signal on Windows."}, "locations": [{"physicalLocation": {"artifactLocation": {"uri": "tests/test_mcp_jobs.py"}, "region": {"startLine": 610, "endLine": 610}}}], "properties": {"source": "L1"}}, {"ruleId": "5404526cf749c32d", "level": "error", "message": {"text": "[adversarial] `_run_review` reads only the tail of stderr (64KB). If the child writes a large amount of output before logging an infra-failure message (e.g., a connection error at the beginning of a long trace), the error may be truncated and `_is_infra_failure` could miss it, causing the entry to be incorrectly marked as HOLD instead of SKIPPED. The design assumes errors always appear at the tail, which is not guaranteed."}, "locations": [{"physicalLocation": {"artifactLocation": {"uri": "src/code_forge/eval/runner.py"}, "region": {"startLine": 164, "endLine": 164}}}], "properties": {"source": "L1"}}], "properties": {"tokenCost": {"inputTokens": 148016, "outputTokens": 31991, "totalTokens": 180007, "backend": "deepseek", "model": "sn-deepseek-flash", "passes": 3, "durationSeconds": 919.9}, "advisories": [{"id": "runtime-0", "axis": "RUNTIME", "file": "src/code